RISC Project Oberon (2013) for OberonStation - Build Summary
Paul Reed 27.10.2015 rev. A

The following steps are a quick reference to build the RISC Project 
Oberon (2013) system from scratch, in the form in which it was developed for
the OberonStation board.  Since the board FPGA is supplied pre-programmed, 
these hardware steps have already been done for you; if you do not have a
pre-programmed microSD-Card then begin at step 1.

This summary assumes familiarity with all relevant software tools and 
hardware, and their own documentation.

Pre-requisites
 - OberonStation board http://oberonstation.x10.mx/
 - microSD-card, PS/2 keyboard, VGA monitor (>= 1024x768)
 - PS/2 mouse with 3 buttons (or with scrollwheel button)
 - PC running Microsoft Windows or Linux (or MacOS if starting at step 1)
 - Xilinx ISE (e.g. WebPack) software (for step 0)
 - JTAG programming cable (for step 0)
 - Internet access, in particular to:
     http://www.inf.ethz.ch/personal/wirth/ProjectOberon
     http://www.projectoberon.com
     http://oberonstation.x10.mx/

Step 0. (Optional) Synthesize hardware and program FPGA
 - download OStationVerilog.zip from http://www.projectoberon.com
 - in Xilinx ISE create new project RISC5 for xc3s700an-fgg484-4
 - unzip OStationVerilog.zip in RISC5 folder (creates src/ subdir)
 - back in ISE, add src/ files to project
 - (optional) cross-compile e.g. BootLoad.Mod to update prom.mem boot code
 - make sure RISC5Top.OStation.v is selected as top module
 - generate programming file (bitfile) RISC5Top.bit (time-consuming)
 - connect JTAG cable and apply USB power
 - write bitfile to FPGA flash using Xilinx iMPACT tool

Step 1. (Optional) Download and write SD-card image
 - download RISCimg.zip raw filesystem image from http://www.projectoberon.com
 - unzip and write raw image (do not simply copy file) to microSD-card
   (Unix/Linux/Mac dd command or a Windows SD-card image raw-writing program)
 - insert microSD-card in socket on OberonStation

Step 2. Plug in hardware and go!
 - ensure OberonStation board is powered-off
 - ensure jumpers J0 and J3 are absent (open) for standard configuration
 - connect VGA monitor, PS/2 keyboard and mouse (in that order) to board
 - cold-start system by plugging in USB power

